<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsugaru Nobumasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1646">1646</a>–<a href="/years/1710">1710</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsugaru Nobumasa was the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of the Hirosaki Domain in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, ruling from 1656 until his death in 1710. A student of Confucian and military learning under Yamaga Sokō, he reformed the domain's administration and finances, opened new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields, promoted irrigation, forestry and crafts, and invited scholars and artisans to Hirosaki. He is remembered as one of the model reforming lords of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsugaru-nobumasa.jpg" alt="Tsugaru Nobumasa" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 新井寒竹 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsugaru_Nobumasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
